---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Ambiguous = `I put the same amount away every week and I want to know
how many weeks until I hit my goal.`;

const Questions = `Questions this brief does not answer

  - Does the last week land exactly on the goal, or overshoot?
  - Do I report the goal amount, or what I actually have?
  - What if I already have enough saved before week 1?
  - What if the weekly amount is zero? (Then it never ends.)

MY DECISIONS
  - Report the real amount, overshoot included.
  - If already at the goal, say so and do not loop.
  - Refuse a weekly amount of zero rather than hang.`;

const pool = [
  { slug: "cc-countdown", title: "Countdown", stars: 1, after: "1.17" },
  { slug: "cc-savings-goal", title: "Savings Goal", stars: 2, after: "1.17" },
  { slug: "cc-times-table", title: "Times Table", stars: 2, after: "1.19" },
  { slug: "cc-fizz-buzz", title: "Fizz Buzz", stars: 2, after: "1.19" },
  { slug: "cc-step-tracker", title: "Step Tracker", stars: 2, after: "1.20" },
  { slug: "cc-guessing-game", title: "Guessing Game", stars: 3, after: "1.20" },
  { slug: "cc-open-loop", title: "Open Brief — Your Own Program", stars: 3, after: "1.20" },
  { slug: "cc-seating-chart", title: "Seating Chart", stars: 3, after: "1.21" },
  { slug: "cc-star-triangle", title: "Star Triangle", stars: 2, after: "1.21" },
];

const bonus = [
  { slug: "cc-prime-check", title: "Prime Check", stars: 3, after: "1.19" },
  { slug: "cc-multiplication-grid", title: "Multiplication Grid", stars: 3, after: "1.21" },
];
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Code Challenge Set 3</h1>

        <h4 class="text-sn-theme">Complete any 5 of the 9 below.</h4>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>What This Set Is For</h2>

          <p>Everything here repeats something. Your program does the same work over and over until a condition says stop &mdash; that is <b>iteration</b>, the third of the three building blocks every algorithm is made of.</p>

          <p>
            Same rule as always: <b>write your requirements list before you
            write any code</b>, and submit both.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="theme">
          <h2>This Set Is About Knowing When to Stop</h2>

          <p>In Set 1 the missing piece was usually a number. In Set 2 it was a boundary. Here it is almost always <b>the stopping condition</b> &mdash; and briefs almost never state it.</p>

          <Code
            code={Ambiguous}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="From the Savings Goal brief"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            That is a normal way for a person to describe what they want, and it
            is not enough to write a program from. Here is what should end up in
            your requirements list:
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Questions}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="Requirements — the questions half"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <p class="mt-4"><b>That fourth question is the one that matters.</b> A loop whose condition can never become false does not give you a wrong answer &mdash; it gives you no answer, forever. Every challenge in this set has at least one input that would do that, and finding it before you run the program is the skill.</p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="challenges">
          <h2>The Challenges</h2>

          <p>Pick <b>any five</b>.</p>

          <ul class="list-unstyled mt-4">
            {
              pool.map((c) => (
                <li class="mb-2">
                  <i class="si-terminal text-sn-theme me-2"></i>
                  <a href={`/courses/ap-csp/unit-1/${c.slug}`}>{c.title}</a>
                  <span class="ms-2">
                    {Array.from({ length: c.stars }).map(() => (
                      <i class="si-star text-sn-green"></i>
                    ))}
                  </span>
                  <span class="text-body-secondary ms-2">
                    &mdash; after Activity {c.after}
                  </span>
                </li>
              ))
            }
          </ul>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="start-here">
          <h2>If the Part 3 quiz Went Badly</h2>

          <p>
            These three are the repair kit. <b>They count toward your five</b> &mdash;
            this is not extra work, it is a different order to do it in.
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li><a href="/courses/ap-csp/unit-1/cc-countdown">Countdown</a> &mdash; Q7 or Q10 — infinite loops</li>
            <li><a href="/courses/ap-csp/unit-1/cc-step-tracker">Step Tracker</a> &mdash; Q8 — the running-max pattern</li>
            <li><a href="/courses/ap-csp/unit-1/cc-seating-chart">Seating Chart</a> &mdash; Q6 — nested loops multiply</li>
          </ul>

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            If the quiz went fine, ignore this section and pick whatever looks
            interesting.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="bonus">
          <h2>If Five Is Not Enough</h2>

          <p>
            These two are <b>extra credit</b>, worth 3 points each on top of the
            set. They are harder than anything in the pool and they are meant to
            be.
          </p>

          <ul class="list-unstyled mt-4">
            {
              bonus.map((c) => (
                <li class="mb-2">
                  <i class="si-flask text-sn-yellow me-2"></i>
                  <a href={`/courses/ap-csp/unit-1/${c.slug}`}>{c.title}</a>
                  <span class="ms-2">
                    {Array.from({ length: c.stars }).map(() => (
                      <i class="si-star text-sn-green"></i>
                    ))}
                  </span>
                  <span class="text-body-secondary ms-2">
                    &mdash; after Activity {c.after}
                  </span>
                </li>
              ))
            }
          </ul>

          <p class="mt-3"><b>Multiplication Grid is harder than it looks.</b> Producing 144 numbers is the easy half; getting them into straight columns with no formatting tools is the challenge.</p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>For <b>each</b> challenge you complete, submit:</p>

          <ol>
            <li>
              <b>Your requirements list</b> &mdash; including anything the brief
              left open and the decision you made about it.
            </li>
            <li><b>Your program file.</b></li>
          </ol>

          <p>
            This set is worth <b>10 points</b>, graded on completion. Extra credit
            is on top and does not replace any of the five.
          </p>
        </section>

        <h1
          class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
        >
          Commence Challenges
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
